  "notes": "Long-form progressive and electro house, deliberately outside the pop-drop economy. His tempo is one of the few in this section documented as a number rather than a band — \"most of his songs are also at a tempo of 128 bpm\" — so the range is narrow and the mode is 128. ⚠ INFERRED: 128 is the only tempo the entry gives, so the ±2 either side of it is playing room the generator needs, not a documented span. The entry then states what he does *not* do, and that is the most useful parameter in it: intensity is \"built purely by adding and subtracting layers across 8-, 16- and 32-bar phrases; almost no snare-roll or riser vocabulary\". So the roll frequency drops to 0.02, the snare-roll ladder is off, there are no riser or impact lanes, and the arrangement runs 32-bar sections with \"very long intros\" and \"a genuine climax rather than a drop\". The melodic identity is \"arpeggiated, slowly-evolving synth sequences — the melody is a process, not a hook\", which is a long arc over 8- and 16-bar phrases with an arpeggiated countermelody at a 0.9 density; the tracker lineage he names (Impulse Tracker before FL Studio) is why the sequencing reads as pattern rather than phrase. Harmony has \"slow harmonic motion; a single progression may run for minutes with the melody evolving over it\", so `maxChords` is 3 and the harmonic rhythm is `vamp`-weighted. The bass is \"a sidechained saw or square, often a rolling 16th arpeggio that doubles as the harmonic content\" — the lowest root probability of the progressive-house block. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc densities, the cell shapes and every register. ⚠ **`chords.harmonicRhythm` re-weighted (2026-08-17, harmony variety floor).** The entry's harmony line is \"minor-mode and modal loops with slow harmonic motion; ... a single progression may run for minutes with the melody evolving over it\", and the engine's `vamp` value is *one* chord held for the whole clip (`generators::chords`: \"One chord for the whole pattern\") — so a `vamp`-dominant weighting rendered a **one**-chord bed the research nowhere describes. The progression now moves at `2_bars_per_chord`, with `vamp` as the minority. `1_per_bar` is deliberately **not** authored: \"slow harmonic motion\" is the entry's own phrase, and two bars a chord is the slowest rate that still moves.",
